"Counts paired observations within a rectangular numeric grid to show a joint distribution.\n\nUse when: Both variables can be divided into meaningful numeric intervals. A dense scatterplot needs explicit, auditable rectangular aggregation.\n\nAvoid when: Bin boundaries would obscure crucial individual observations or curved boundaries dominate interpretation.\n\nVisual grammar: Cell position and extent encode the two numeric intervals. Cell color encodes count, probability mass, or a declared density.\n\nBuild: Choose and disclose numeric bin boundaries on both axes. Count each paired observation into one rectangular cell. Draw true cell extents with a labeled color scale and explicit missing-data treatment.\n\nCheck: Check total counts equal the included paired sample size. Verify equal-area cells if color represents comparable counts. Use count divided by cell area when unequal bins must encode density.\n\nPitfalls: Empty cells and unobserved cells have different meanings. Color saturation can conceal differences among dense cells; use a stated logarithmic count scale if justified.\n\nExample: Plot synthetic order weight versus delivery time in fixed rectangular bins, with fill representing order count.\n\nAlternatives: Hexbin plot, Scatterplot, Density contour plot."
